### What is a Min Stack?

A stack that supports push, pop, top, and getMin in O(1). A plain stack would need O(n) to scan for the minimum. The two-stack approach trades O(n) extra space for O(1) getMin.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class MinStackO1 {
    private final Deque<Integer> main = new ArrayDeque<>();
    private final Deque<Integer> mins = new ArrayDeque<>();

    public void push(int val) {
        main.push(val);
        mins.push(mins.isEmpty() ? val : Math.min(val, mins.peek()));
    }

    public void pop() { main.pop(); mins.pop(); }
    public int top() { return main.peek(); }
    public int getMin() { return mins.peek(); }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MinStackO1 ms = new MinStackO1();
        ms.push(5); ms.push(1); ms.push(7);
        System.out.println("Min: " + ms.getMin()); // 1
        ms.pop(); // pop 7
        System.out.println("Min: " + ms.getMin()); // 1
        ms.pop(); // pop 1
        System.out.println("Min: " + ms.getMin()); // 5
    }
}
```

```text
Min: 1
Min: 1
Min: 5
```

### How does lockstep popping maintain correctness?

At each level, the auxiliary entry is the minimum of everything below and including that level. Popping both stacks removes the latest entry and reveals the previous minimum, which was correct for the earlier state. No recomputation is needed.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class LockstepDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Deque<Integer> main = new ArrayDeque<>();
        Deque<Integer> mins = new ArrayDeque<>();
        int[] vals = {10, 3, 8, 1};

        for (int v : vals) {
            main.push(v);
            mins.push(mins.isEmpty() ? v : Math.min(v, mins.peek()));
            System.out.println("Push " + v + " -> min=" + mins.peek());
        }
        System.out.println();
        while (!main.isEmpty()) {
            int v = main.pop();
            int m = mins.pop();
            System.out.println("Pop " + v + " -> previous min=" + (mins.isEmpty() ? "empty" : mins.peek()));
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Push 10 -> min=10
Push 3 -> min=3
Push 8 -> min=3
Push 1 -> min=1

Pop 1 -> previous min=3
Pop 8 -> previous min=3
Pop 3 -> previous min=10
Pop 10 -> previous min=empty
```

### How do you use BigDecimal for banking?

Money comparisons use `compareTo`. Push the smaller of the new value and the current minimum onto the auxiliary stack using `<= 0` for the comparison.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class MinStackBD {
    private final Deque<BigDecimal> main = new ArrayDeque<>();
    private final Deque<BigDecimal> mins = new ArrayDeque<>();

    public void push(BigDecimal val) {
        main.push(val);
        BigDecimal cur = mins.isEmpty() ? val
                : (val.compareTo(mins.peek()) <= 0 ? val : mins.peek());
        mins.push(cur);
    }

    public BigDecimal getMin() { return mins.peek(); }
    public void pop() { main.pop(); mins.pop(); }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MinStackBD ms = new MinStackBD();
        ms.push(new BigDecimal("50000"));
        ms.push(new BigDecimal("30000"));
        ms.push(new BigDecimal("80000"));
        System.out.println("Lowest: " + ms.getMin());
    }
}
```

```text
Lowest: 30000
```

### What is the space-optimized variant?

Push to the min stack only when `val <= mins.peek()`. Pop from it only when `main.pop().equals(mins.peek())`. Using `<=` ensures duplicate minimums are each tracked.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class MinStackOpt {
    private final Deque<Integer> main = new ArrayDeque<>();
    private final Deque<Integer> mins = new ArrayDeque<>();

    public void push(int val) {
        main.push(val);
        if (mins.isEmpty() || val <= mins.peek()) mins.push(val);
    }

    public void pop() {
        if (main.pop().equals(mins.peek())) mins.pop();
    }

    public int getMin() { return mins.peek(); }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MinStackOpt ms = new MinStackOpt();
        ms.push(3); ms.push(3); ms.push(5);
        ms.pop();
        System.out.println("Min: " + ms.getMin()); // 3
        ms.pop();
        System.out.println("Min: " + ms.getMin()); // 3
    }
}
```

```text
Min: 3
Min: 3
```

### Why does <= matter for duplicate minimums?

Strict `<` creates only one min entry for duplicate minimums. Popping one copy removes that entry, leaving the other copy without a matching min record. The `<=` condition creates an entry per copy.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class DuplicateMinBug {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("< : push 3,3 -> mins=[3]. Pop 3 -> mins=[]. Bug for second 3!");
        System.out.println("<= : push 3,3 -> mins=[3,3]. Pop 3 -> mins=[3]. Correct!");
    }
}
```

```text
< : push 3,3 -> mins=[3]. Pop 3 -> mins=[]. Bug for second 3!
<= : push 3,3 -> mins=[3,3]. Pop 3 -> mins=[3]. Correct!
```

### What are common mistakes?

Using `<` instead of `<=` in the optimized variant. Forgetting to pop both stacks in the simple version. Using `==` on `Integer` objects beyond the cached range. Not handling the empty-stack case on the first push.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class MinStackBugs {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Bug 1: strict < in optimized -> duplicate min lost");
        System.out.println("Bug 2: pop only main, forget mins -> drift");
        System.out.println("Bug 3: == on Integer > 127 -> reference comparison fails");
        System.out.println("Bug 4: peek empty mins on first push -> exception");
    }
}
```

```text
Bug 1: strict < in optimized -> duplicate min lost
Bug 2: pop only main, forget mins -> drift
Bug 3: == on Integer > 127 -> reference comparison fails
Bug 4: peek empty mins on first push -> exception
```

### How is this applied in banking?

At National Bank of India, the trading desk stages and cancels pending order amounts on a stack, and the risk monitor needs the smallest pending amount at every instant to enforce a floor rule. The min stack answers in O(1) even as orders churn rapidly.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class TradingRiskMonitor {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MinStackBD desk = new MinStackBD();
        desk.push(new BigDecimal("100000"));
        desk.push(new BigDecimal("50000"));
        desk.push(new BigDecimal("75000"));
        System.out.println("Smallest pending: " + desk.getMin());
        desk.pop();
        System.out.println("After cancel: " + desk.getMin());
    }
}
```

```text
Smallest pending: 50000
After cancel: 50000
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Three approaches: two stacks in lockstep (O(n) space, clear), push-only-on-new-min (less space, tricky pop), and delta encoding (O(1) extra, hard to read). Present the clean two-stack version in interviews and mention the others to show depth.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class MinStackSenior {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("1. Two stacks lockstep: O(n) extra, clear");
        System.out.println("2. Push on <=: less space, tricky pop logic");
        System.out.println("3. Delta encoding: O(1) extra, sacrifices clarity");
        System.out.println("All keep every operation O(1)");
    }
}
```

```text
1. Two stacks lockstep: O(n) extra, clear
2. Push on <=: less space, tricky pop logic
3. Delta encoding: O(1) extra, sacrifices clarity
All keep every operation O(1)
```
